/// db/schema.lite — THE SEED.
///
/// One shop. Everything below is derived from this file, at build time or at
/// runtime, and restated nowhere:
///
///   the tables                 ← autoMigrate(db)
///   CRUD + pagination          ← createBaseService({}) reads the model
///   401 / 403                  ← @@gate, graded by api/gate.ts
///   400 on bad input           ← the field rules, via junction's autoValidate
///   which state changes exist  ← @@transitions, enforced at the Data boundary
///   make() defaults            ← generateJsonSchema → registerSchemas → browser
///   fields / enum options      ← the same document, $ref resolved client-side
///   relation pickers           ← x-relations
///   which buttons to render    ← x-gate / x-transitions
///
/// Model names are PascalCase and singular — ALWAYS.
///
/// WHAT IS NOT IN THIS FILE: the four auth models (User, Credential, Session,
/// Verification). api/db.ts appends them from `authSchemaFragments()` at
/// startup rather than pasting a copy here, so there is one definition of them
/// in the repo instead of two. The browser never sees them, which is correct —
/// the three credential-material models are @@gate("8"), and `User` reads at
/// USER(4) with a row policy and two field policies bounding it: 8 is for a
/// model nothing outside auth has anything to say to, not for identity.

// ─── Tenancy ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// MANY SHOPS, one file each. A shop is the tenant, and the isolation is the
// filesystem rather than a column: two shops cannot see each other's orders
// because there is no query that reaches both, and taking a backup of one shop
// is copying a file.
//
// `strategy row` would have been the other answer, and it is the wrong one
// here. A shop is not a workspace inside one company's product — it is a
// separate business with its own staff, its own customers and its own money,
// and the failure mode of a missing row policy is one shop reading another's
// orders. Under this strategy that failure cannot be expressed.
//
// **The people are per shop too**, which follows from the same file: `User`,
// `Credential`, `Session` and `Verification` are appended into every tenant's
// database, so an account at one shop is not an account at another. That is
// what makes a sign-in a question about WHICH shop before it is a question
// about who — see `api/src/core/auth.ts`.
//
// `resolve subdomain` is the deployment fact: `highstreet.shop.test` is the
// High Street shop. There is no default here, deliberately — which of a host, a
// header and a claim names the tenant is something nothing can infer — and the
// app's own answer for a request that names none is in `api/src/core/db.ts`.
//
// `dir` and `registry` are declared for the CLI's benefit (`litestone tenant
// list`, run from db/) and passed explicitly by the app, which assembles its
// schema in memory and so has no file to resolve a relative path against.
tenancy {
  strategy database
  dir      "./shops"
  registry "./shops-registry.db"
  maxOpen  25
  resolve  subdomain
}

// ─── Databases ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `main` is where everything lands: a model with no @@db goes here, and auth's
// fragments name it explicitly. NOTE the sharp edge in a schema with no
// tenancy — once `database main` is declared THIS PATH WINS and createClient's
// `db:` option is ignored entirely, so `db: ':memory:'` would silently write
// ./db/shop.db and you would not be told.
//
// Under `tenancy { strategy database }` the path below is never used: the
// registry redirects every sqlite database to the tenant's own file, because a
// declared path sends every shop to one database, which is the isolation hole
// this whole block exists to close. It stays declared because a schema still
// needs to say where `main` is for every reader that is not a fleet — the CLI,
// Studio, the browser build.
database main {
  path env("SHOP_DB_PATH", "./db/shop.db")
}

// @@log(audit) writes here, and this is the ONE database a tenant registry does
// not redirect — a logger database is shared across the whole fleet by design,
// so it is the one whose declared path is used as written.
//
// Three scripts here `cd <surface>` before running, and this path is read from
// the APP ROOT regardless: the CLI anchors to it from the schema file, and
// api/src/core/db.ts names that file to `createTenantRegistry` so the string it
// assembled in memory resolves the same way (`FJS-449`). Before that it
// followed the process CWD, which is how an orphan `example/site/db/` came to
// exist and sit there for two days, unnoticed because the repo ignores `*.db*`
// everywhere.
database audit {
  path      env("AUDIT_PATH", "./db/audit/")
  driver    logger
  retention 90d
}

// ─── Removal and revision ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two Data-boundary features declared here and restated in no service and no
// component. Both are about a SECOND writer.
//
// **@@softDelete** — Customer, Order (+ OrderLine) and Product (+
// ProductVariant, ProductImage). Every one is a row something else in this file
// points AT, so destroying it destroys history belonging to somebody other than
// the person who pressed the button. The ledger is the proof that this is not
// hypothetical: `InventoryMovement.variant` is `onDelete: Restrict` precisely so
// a shelf that has moved cannot be deleted out from under its own tape — which
// meant that a product which had ever sold could not be taken out of the
// catalogue AT ALL. The refusal was right and there was no other way out.
//
// It is NOT a second `active`. `active: false` is *we are not selling this right
// now*: the row stays in the catalogue, staff see it, it comes back for next
// season. `deletedAt` is *this is not ours any more*. What makes them different
// rather than merely differently spelled is `@unique` — a deleted product keeps
// its slug, deliberately, because a way back that fails when a stranger has
// taken the value is not a way back. So re-using a deleted product's name is a
// 409 naming the row holding it, and freeing the slot is a decision the app
// makes (move the value, or stop keeping the row).
//
// **@version** — Product, Customer, ShippingMethod, TaxRate. Every one is a row
// a FORM edits and nothing else writes. Two people on one both PATCH, and
// without the column the second silently erases the first.
//
// The rest are left out on purpose, because @version is per ROW and not per
// column: where two writers touch DISJOINT columns it reports a conflict about a
// change nobody made, and a 409 nobody can act on teaches everyone to retry
// blindly.
//
//   ProductVariant     a person edits the price; every sale, delivery and
//                      stocktake writes `stock`. Those two never collide, and
//                      the column would say that they had
//   Order              @@transitions(status, …) is already a compare-and-swap on
//                      the one column two writers contend for, and it refuses BY
//                      NAME; `trackingCode` is written by the courier job.
//                      @version on top refuses a second time for a reason nobody
//                      asked about
//   Discount           `redemptions` is incremented inside every checkout
//   Cart · CartLine    one shopper, one basket. There is no second editor
//   OrderLine · InventoryMovement · PaymentEvent
//                      append-only — written once, never edited

// ─── Catalogue ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// Which of the three a piece of merch carries. A brand is a small fixed set
/// the shop does not invent, so it is an enum; a Collection — the thing a
/// merchant makes up and edits — is a table, and is not this.
enum Brand { frontierjs junction litestone }

/// The sizes stocked. `one` is not a filler member: a mug and a sticker
/// genuinely have one size, and a NULLABLE size would take the composite
/// @@unique on ProductVariant down with it. SQLite counts two NULLs as
/// distinct, so `(1, NULL, NULL)` inserts twice and two rows end up sharing
/// one shelf. Measured — litestone refuses @@unique over an @encrypted member
/// by name and says nothing about a nullable one.
enum Size { one xs s m l xl xxl }

/// A Product is the FAMILY, not the thing with a price on it. "FrontierJS Tee"
/// is a product; the navy one in medium is what a person puts in a basket and
/// what a warehouse counts down.
///
/// That split is why `sku`, `price` and `barcode` are NOT on this model. They
/// were, while every product was one buyable thing — and the moment one design
/// carried four colourways, a price on the family had no answer.
model Product {
  id   Int    @id
  name String @length(1, 80) @unique

  /// The storefront URL segment. A real column rather than @generated from the
  /// name, because a slug must SURVIVE a rename: a link somebody bookmarked is
  /// not something the catalogue may rewrite. @generated would also make it
  /// readOnly, and the merchant is the one who decides it.
  slug String @length(1, 80) @unique @lower

  description String? @length(0, 2000)
  brand       Brand

  active    Boolean  @default(true)
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// Not a second `active`, and the difference is which question it answers.
  /// `active: false` is *we are not selling this right now* — it stays in the
  /// catalogue, staff see it, it comes back for next season. This is *it is not
  /// ours any more*, and until it existed the catalogue had no way to say so:
  /// `InventoryMovement.variant` is `onDelete: Restrict`, so a product that had
  /// ever sold one unit could not be deleted, correctly and with no way out.
  ///
  /// `(cascade)` below, because the variants and the photographs are this
  /// product and there is nothing they mean without it.
  deletedAt DateTime?

  /// Two merchants editing one product. Without this the second save silently
  /// erases the first; with it the second is a 409 carrying both revisions, and
  /// a screen can offer the choice the person would have wanted.
  ///
  /// Here and not on `ProductVariant` for the reason at the top of this file:
  /// nothing but a person writes a column on this row.
  version Int @version

  variants ProductVariant[]
  images   ProductImage[]

  /// What the variants add up to, answered by SQLite on the row rather than by
  /// the screen after it.
  ///
  /// The products list computed all four of these in the browser, which meant
  /// loading every variant of every product to show a column — fine at twelve
  /// SKUs and wrong at twelve thousand, and the shape that makes a list page
  /// slow long before anybody profiles it. A `@from` is a correlated subquery:
  /// it costs no extra round trip, it is not a column, and it cannot go stale
  /// because there is nothing cached to invalidate.
  ///
  /// `priceFrom` / `priceTo` are the range the catalogue prints as `£10–£14`,
  /// and they are the same two numbers whether one variant exists or twelve —
  /// equal when there is nothing to choose between.
  ///
  /// `onHand` is the sum of the shelves and NOT what is buyable: available is
  /// stock minus the unexpired holds, which lives in `api/inventory.ts` for the
  /// reason written on `ProductVariant.stock`. A shopper is never shown this
  /// number.
  variantCount Int   @from(ProductVariant, count: true)
  priceFrom    Int   @from(ProductVariant, min: price) @money(USD)
  priceTo      Int   @from(ProductVariant, max: price) @money(USD)
  onHand       Int   @from(ProductVariant, sum: stock)

  /// What a person is shown when a picker offers one of these. Without it the
  /// scan picks the first plain string — here `name`, correctly, but by luck.
  @@label(name)

  /// The catalogue is searchable, and it is searchable on the SERVER.
  ///
  /// This is the one thing a prerendered storefront cannot do for itself. The
  /// catalogue island is handed the products that were baked into the page, so
  /// what it can filter is what was baked — which is every product in a shop
  /// this size and a page of them in a shop that is not. A search box that
  /// silently means *of the ones on this page* is the kind of wrong that looks
  /// right until the shop grows.
  ///
  /// Two columns and not three. `brand` is an enum and matching it is an
  /// ordinary equality filter; putting it in the index would make a search for
  /// `junction` rank a brand match against a name match with nothing saying
  /// which it did. SKUs are not here either, and cannot be: they live on
  /// `ProductVariant`, and an FTS index spans one table.
  @@fts([name, description])

  @@softDelete(cascade)

  @@gate("0.4.4.5")
}

/// The colourways this shop has run. `ProductVariant.colour` stores the NAME
/// rather than a foreign key, because the value has to outlive the row: a
/// colourway that is retired is still what the navy tees in the warehouse are,
/// and a deleted row must not take that answer with it.
model Colour {
  id         Int       @id
  name       String    @length(1, 30) @unique
  
  /// For the swatch a picker draws. Optional — a colourway added by a merchant
  /// typing it into the variant form has no hex until somebody sets one.
  hex        String?   @length(4, 7)
  
  /// Out of the offered list, still on every variant that ran in it. This is
  /// what @@scope below reads, and it is why the set is a table and not an enum:
  /// retiring a colourway is a row edit rather than a deploy.
  retired    Boolean   @default(false)
  
  createdAt  DateTime  @default(now())
  
  /// What a picker shows, and — because `value` and this are the same column —
  /// the column an `open` binding writes what the merchant typed into.
  @@label(name)
  
  /// The narrowing the value set names. It travels to the browser as a filter,
  /// so the list the picker offers is the list the Data boundary will accept.
  @@scope(current, retired == false)
  
  @@gate("0.4.4.5")
}

/// The list behind `ProductVariant.colour`. `value name` rather than the id is
/// the whole point: what is stored is the colourway, readable on its own, and
/// the row is the metadata about it.
valueset ProductColour {
  source Colour
  value  name
  scope  current
}

/// The buyable thing. One row per option combination, and the row a basket
/// line, a price and a stock count all point at.
model ProductVariant {
  id            Int                  @id
  productId     Int                  @label("Product") @required("Every variant belongs to a product")
  product       Product              @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  
  sku           String               @length(3, 20) @unique @upper
  
  /// The option columns. NOT nullable, and the @@unique below is the whole
  /// reason — see the note on `enum Size`. A product with nothing to choose
  /// takes the defaults, which is what Shopify spells "Default Title".
  ///
  /// `colour` is the pair `size` is not: a stored STRING with a declared list
  /// behind it. `open` is what makes it a colourway rather than an enum — the
  /// merchant picks one the shop has run before, and typing a new one adds it
  /// to the list instead of asking for a schema change and a deploy.
  colour        String               @length(1, 30) @default("Default") @values(ProductColour, open)
  size          Size                 @default(one)
  
  price         Int                  @money(USD) @gte(0)
  barcode       String?              @unique
  
  /// ON HAND: what the warehouse could physically pick right now. NOT what is
  /// buyable — a basket holding a reservation has not taken this down yet, and
  /// a shopper who is offered the last one after somebody else is already
  /// holding it finds out at the till, which is the worst place to find out.
  ///
  /// AVAILABLE = stock − the unexpired holds. It is not a column and cannot be:
  /// it is a sum over another table that changes with the clock, and a column
  /// would be a cached answer with nothing to invalidate it. `api/inventory.ts`
  /// is the one place it is computed and the one place this column is written.
  stock         Int                  @default(0) @gte(0)
  
  active        Boolean              @default(true)
  createdAt     DateTime             @default(now())
  
  /// Stamped by `Product`'s cascade, and reachable on its own for a colourway
  /// that was a mistake. The ledger and the holds hanging off this row are not
  /// touched by either: they belong to the shelf, and what physically moved did
  /// not stop having happened.
  deletedAt     DateTime?
  
  images        ProductImage[]
  /// The holds against this shelf, and every reason it ever moved.
  reservations  StockReservation[]
  movements     InventoryMovement[]
  
  /// One row per combination. Without it a second "navy / m" is a legal insert
  /// and the two rows fight over one shelf.
  @@unique([productId, colour, size])
  
  /// A picker over variants shows the SKU — the option columns are two fields
  /// and a label field is one, and a SKU is what the shop floor says out loud.
  @@label(sku)
  
  /// The storefront never asks for a variant it cannot sell: the product page,
  /// the buy box and the catalogue all read `{ productId, active: true }`, and
  /// the prerender runs that once per product. A plain index on `productId`
  /// carries every retired colourway the shop has ever run, which is the half
  /// nothing reads. The predicate is ANDed with `@@softDelete`'s own clause, so
  /// the index holds live active rows and the caller's query — which litestone
  /// narrows to live rows for them — matches it exactly.
  @@index([productId], where: active == true)
  
  /// `(cascade)` reaches the photographs of THIS colourway and nothing else — a
  /// flat-lay of the whole range names no variant, so its foreign key is null
  /// and it is not matched. The holds and the ledger below are not soft-delete
  /// tables at all, so the cascade cannot touch them by construction.
  @@softDelete(cascade)
  
  @@gate("0.4.4.5")
  @@log(audit)
}

/// A photograph. The bytes live in object storage and this column holds the
/// reference — `File` is the type that means that, and `FileStorage` in
/// api/db.ts is what turns a path or an upload into one.
model ProductImage {
  id         Int              @id
  productId  Int              @label("Product")
  product    Product          @relation(fields: [productId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  
  /// Null means "shows the family". A colourway shot names its variant, and a
  /// flat-lay of the whole range names none.
  variantId  Int?             @label("Variant")
  variant    ProductVariant?  @relation(fields: [variantId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  
  file       File             @accept("image/png, image/jpeg, image/webp")
  alt        String           @length(1, 160) @label("Alt text")
  @required("Describe the photo — a shop with no alt text is a shop half its customers cannot use")
  
  position   Int              @default(0)
  
  createdAt  DateTime         @default(now())
  
  /// Stamped by `Product`'s cascade. The bytes are not deleted with the row —
  /// a restored product with no photographs is a product nobody will buy.
  deletedAt  DateTime?
  
  @@softDelete
  
  @@gate("0.4.4.5")
}

// ─── Customers ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

model Customer {
  id          Int        @id
  name        String     @length(1, 80)
  firstName   String     @length(1, 80)
  lastName    String     @length(1, 80)
  fullName    String?    @generated(`{firstName} {lastName}`)
  email       String     @email @unique @lower
  
  /// Staff-only. Field-level @allow is the tool for "some callers may read this
  /// column": the field is stripped from the response for anyone the policy
  /// rejects, without a line of service code saying so.
  ///
  /// NOT @guarded — that is a system-context lock (@guarded / @guarded(all)),
  /// not a level, and it takes no numeric argument. @guarded(5) does not parse.
  notes       String?    @allow('read', auth().role == 'admin')
  
  /// The credential behind this record, once there is one.
  ///
  /// A `Customer` is the SHOP's record of a person — it exists the moment
  /// somebody checks out as a guest, with no password and no way back in. A
  /// `User` is the credential. They are two facts and the second is optional,
  /// which is why this is nullable and why it is here rather than the other way
  /// round: every buyer has a record, and only some of them have an account.
  ///
  /// `@unique` because two accounts claiming one customer record is two people
  /// reading each other's orders. Written by the app — at checkout when the
  /// caller is signed in, and at registration when an address already has a
  /// history — never by the caller, which is what `@system` says.
  userId      String?    @unique @system
  
  /// `@keep` is the whole of the paragraph below, said in one word: when this
  /// row is soft-deleted these do NOT go with it. Without it the schema warns
  /// that the children were left live — which is what forgetting the cascade
  /// looks like, and here it is the intention.
  orders      Order[]    @keep
  createdAt   DateTime   @default(now())
  
  /// How many orders this person has, answered by SQLite on the row.
  ///
  /// It is on screen because it is what makes `@keep` VISIBLE rather than
  /// merely true: a removed customer still reads 2, and had the orders been
  /// cascaded it would read 0 with nothing on the page saying which happened.
  /// A `@from` reads its target the way the target is read, so this counts LIVE
  /// orders — an order removed on its own does come off it.
  orderCount  Int        @from(Order, count: true)
  
  /// ─── Removal, and why the orders do not go with it ──────────────────────
  ///
  /// A customer asks to be taken off the books. Until this column the shop
  /// could not do it: `Order.customerId` is `onDelete: Cascade`, so `remove()`
  /// took every order the person had ever placed with it. An order is a
  /// financial record — it is what the revenue is made of and what an auditor
  /// reads — and losing one because somebody unsubscribed is a bookkeeping
  /// failure wearing a privacy feature's clothes.
  ///
  /// Soft delete is a VISIBILITY rule, so the person leaves every list, every
  /// picker and every search while the rows pointing at them stay where they
  /// are. Plain and deliberately not `(cascade)`, which is the one thing that
  /// would reintroduce the bug.
  ///
  /// `email` is `@unique` and a deleted row keeps it, so a returning customer
  /// is a `restore()` and not a second record — and re-registering the address
  /// against a NEW record is a 409 the app has to answer on purpose.
  deletedAt   DateTime?
  
  /// Two staff editing one customer. `userId` is `@system`, but it is stamped
  /// once when an account is linked and never written again — a one-shot stamp
  /// is not a second editor, which is what keeps this row inside the rule at the
  /// top of the file.
  version     Int        @version
  
  /// Read needs a SIGNED-IN caller, and the policies below decide which rows.
  ///
  /// It was `"0.4.4.5"` — the catalogue's gate, pasted onto the ledger — so
  /// `GET /api/customers` with no token at all answered every customer's name
  /// and email. Four models legitimately read at 0 and they are all catalogue:
  /// the prerendered storefront reads them with no session, which is the whole
  /// of what a public shop front is. This is not one of them.
  ///
  /// 1 rather than 4 because a shopper who has registered and not yet clicked
  /// the link in their email is VISITOR(1), and their own order history is
  /// exactly the thing they should be able to see while they wait.
  @@gate("1.4.4.5")
  
  /// Staff read every customer. `isStaff` is a column on User and NOT a level:
  /// a verified shopper and a member of staff both grade USER(4), so the ladder
  /// cannot tell them apart and was never meant to — a gate answers *what kind
  /// of caller*, and this is *which person*.
  @@allow('read', auth().isStaff)
  
  /// …and a shopper reads their own record and no other.
  @@allow('read', userId == auth().id)
  
  @@softDelete
  
  @@log(audit)
}

// ─── Money ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Three things stand between the sum of the lines and what the card is
// charged, and each one is a different KIND of thing:
//
//   Discount        the shop giving something up, on a code somebody typed
//   ShippingMethod  a service the shop is buying on the shopper's behalf
//   TaxRate         money that was never the shop's, collected for somebody else
//
// They share one property that decides every design below: **the arithmetic
// has exactly one owner**, `api/src/pricing.ts`, and the basket screen, the
// checkout and the receipt all ask it rather than each doing the multiplication
// themselves. A total computed in two places is two totals, and the day they
// disagree is the day a customer is charged a number no screen ever showed.
//
// The second property is that a receipt must EXPLAIN itself. `Order` carries
// nine columns below that are copies of rows here — the code, the wording, the
// rate — for `OrderLine`'s reason: what the shop charges today is one fact and
// what this shopper was charged is another, and re-reading the first to render
// the second rewrites history every time a merchant edits a rate.

/// How a discount reduces a subtotal. Two members and no third: a
/// buy-one-get-one is a rule about LINES and cannot be expressed as a number
/// against the whole, so it would be a different feature wearing this enum's
/// name.
enum DiscountKind { percent fixed }

/// A code somebody types at the till.
///
/// ─── Why a shopper may not read this table ────────────────────────────────
///
/// `@@gate("5.5.5.5")` — staff only, read included. Every other thing a
/// shopper interacts with here is readable at 0, because a storefront has to
/// list it; a discount code is the exception, and the reason is that listing
/// them IS the exploit. `GET /api/discounts` answering the table hands every
/// unreleased code to anyone who asks, and a shop's codes are worth money.
///
/// So the shopper never reads a row: they send a string to
/// `carts.applyDiscount`, which validates it through the SYSTEM client and
/// answers the basket with the money recalculated — the same shape
/// `StockReservation` uses for holds, and for the same reason. What comes back
/// is what this code is worth to THIS basket, which is the only fact about it
/// they are entitled to.
model Discount {
  id Int @id

  /// What is typed. `@upper` so `welcome10` and `WELCOME10` are one code —
  /// a shopper reading a code off a poster is not going to match its case,
  /// and a lookup that fails on that is a support ticket.
  code String @length(3, 20, "A code is 3 to 20 characters") @unique @upper
                @label("Code")

  /// What the basket screen CALLS it. The code is an identifier; this is the
  /// sentence beside the amount, and a receipt saying `-$4.00 (WELCOME10)`
  /// tells a customer less than one saying `-$4.00 (10% off your first order)`.
  label String @length(1, 60) @label("Description")

  kind  DiscountKind @default(percent) @label("Kind")

  /// Percent: 0–100. Fixed: an amount in the shop's base currency. One column
  /// for two units, which is the cost of not having two tables — a FIELD
  /// validator here cannot see `kind`, so the bound that matters is declared
  /// at the model instead, below. `pricing.ts` still caps the discount at the
  /// subtotal, which is a different rule: this one is about what may be
  /// STORED, that one about what may be charged.
  ///
  /// `@scale(2)` and not `@money`, and the two units are why: half the rows
  /// here are not money. What they share is the SCALE — a dollar's minor unit
  /// and a percentage's hundredth are both two places — so one exact integer
  /// column carries both without either reading being a rounded copy of
  /// itself. 1050 is $10.50 under `fixed` and 10.50% under `percent`, and the
  /// bound below is stated in the same units.
  value Int @scale(2) @gte(0) @label("Value")

  /// The floor this code is worth anything at. Zero means none.
  minSubtotal Int @money(USD) @default(0) @gte(0) @label("Minimum spend")

  /// The window. Both nullable — a code with no start is live now, a code with
  /// no end runs until somebody turns it off.
  startsAt DateTime? @label("Starts")
  endsAt   DateTime? @label("Ends")

  /// How many times it may be used, and how many times it has been. Null means
  /// no limit.
  ///
  /// `redemptions` is `@system`: the application writes it, never a caller,
  /// and never a merchant editing the form — a counter a person can set is not
  /// a count of anything. It is incremented inside `carts.checkout`'s
  /// transaction by read-modify-write rather than `{ increment: 1 }`, for
  /// `stock`'s reason: an atomic operator computes the new value inside SQLite
  /// where no validator can see it, so the limit would be passed and the
  /// constraint would never fire (`FJS-D27`). The transaction is what makes
  /// the read-then-write safe, and it is the same transaction the order is
  /// written in — a code redeemed by an order that rolls back was not redeemed.
  maxRedemptions Int? @gte(1) @label("Redemption limit")
  redemptions    Int  @default(0) @gte(0) @system @label("Times used")

  active Boolean @default(true) @label("Active")

  /// The baskets currently carrying it. Not the redemption count — a basket
  /// that is abandoned redeemed nothing, which is why `redemptions` is a
  /// column incremented at checkout rather than a count of these.
  carts Cart[]

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// The limit, enforced where no code path can get around it.
  ///
  /// `carts.service.ts` counts a redemption with a read-modify-write rather than
  /// `{ increment: 1 }` precisely so a validator can see the new value — an
  /// atomic operator computes it inside SQLite, where nothing in this package
  /// runs (`FJS-D27`). This is the other half of that argument: a CHECK is IN
  /// the table, so it fires for the atomic operator too, and for a migration, a
  /// seed, `asSystem()` and `fli tinker`. The service still refuses first and
  /// says something useful; this is what makes the refusal true.
  @@check("maxRedemptions IS NULL OR redemptions <= maxRedemptions",
          "a code cannot be redeemed more times than its limit")

  /// A window that closes before it opens. Two nullable columns of one row,
  /// which is the shape no field validator can see: each is individually fine
  /// and the pair is not.
  @@check("startsAt IS NULL OR endsAt IS NULL OR startsAt < endsAt",
          "a discount must end after it starts")

  /// The bound `value` cannot state on its own, because what it means depends
  /// on `kind` and a field validator sees one field.
  @@check("kind != 'percent' OR value <= 10000",
          "a percentage discount cannot be more than 100%")

  @@gate("5.5.5.5")
  @@log(audit)
}

/// What the shop charges to send it.
///
/// Readable at 0, unlike a discount: a storefront has to OFFER these, and the
/// prices are printed on the shipping page anyway. That difference — one table
/// public, its neighbour staff-only, both on the same screen — is the clearest
/// statement in this schema that a gate answers *what kind of caller* and not
/// *what kind of table*.
model ShippingMethod {
  id           Int      @id
  
  name         String   @length(1, 40) @unique @label("Method")
  description  String?  @length(0, 120) @label("Description")
  
  price        Int      @money(USD) @gte(0) @label("Price")
  
  /// The subtotal at which this method costs nothing. Null means never free.
  ///
  /// Measured against the subtotal AFTER the discount, which is a decision and
  /// not an accident: a shop that gives free shipping over 50 and a code worth
  /// 10 off has to choose whether a 55 basket with the code applied still
  /// qualifies. Discount-first is the answer that cannot surprise a merchant
  /// into shipping for free at a loss, and `pricing.ts` is where it is written
  /// down once.
  freeOver     Int?     @money(USD) @gte(0) @label("Free over")
  
  /// The order they are offered in. A merchant decides which one is at the
  /// top; alphabetical would put Express above Standard and change what most
  /// people pick.
  position     Int      @default(0) @label("Position")
  active       Boolean  @default(true) @label("Active")
  
  carts        Cart[]
  
  /// A rate table two people maintain, and nothing else writes a column on it.
  version      Int      @version
  
  @@gate("0.5.5.5")
}

/// The rate this shop collects, and what it is called where the shop is.
///
/// A row rather than a constant because this is a FLEET: `tenancy { strategy
/// database }` gives every shop its own file, so a shop in one jurisdiction
/// and a shop in another disagree about both numbers — and a constant in the
/// API would be the one fact about a shop that every shop had to share.
///
/// `label` is not decoration. A line on a receipt reading `Tax` where the law
/// says `VAT` is a receipt a business customer cannot file.
model TaxRate {
  id         Int      @id
  
  label      String   @length(1, 30) @label("Shown as")
  
  /// A FRACTION, not a percentage — 0.2 and not 20. One representation, chosen
  /// because it is the one the arithmetic uses; a column storing 20 and a
  /// calculation dividing by 100 puts a unit conversion between the merchant
  /// and the money, and it only has to be forgotten once.
  rate       Float    @gte(0) @lte(1) @label("Rate")
  
  /// Which one applies when nothing else says. Exactly one row should carry
  /// it; `pricing.ts` takes the first active default and charges nothing when
  /// there is none, because a shop that has not configured tax is a shop that
  /// does not charge it — not one that should refuse to sell.
  isDefault  Boolean  @default(false) @label("Default")
  active     Boolean  @default(true)  @label("Active")
  
  /// Same shape as `ShippingMethod`, and the stakes are higher: two people
  /// editing one rate and the second write winning silently is a shop charging
  /// a number nobody chose.
  version    Int      @version
  
  @@gate("0.5.5.5")
}

// ─── Orders ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/// No @default on purpose where the form is concerned: a required enum with no
/// blank member means make() leaves it null and the form has to ask. Order
/// DOES default to `pending`, because an order's initial state is the shop's
/// decision and not the user's.
enum OrderStatus { pending paid shipped refunded cancelled }

/// The payload `orders.recordTracking` accepts — the courier job's way back in.
///
/// A CUSTOM METHOD has no model behind it, so nothing derived `ctx.data` for
/// one: `recordTracking` read `$.data as { trackingCode?: string }` and a cast
/// is not a check. `methods: [{ method, input }]` in the service names this
/// type, and it is validated exactly as a model create is — same generated
/// JSON Schema, same wording, same 400.
///
/// It is a `type` rather than a second model because there is nothing to store:
/// the column lives on Order and this describes only what may arrive.
type TrackingUpdate {
  trackingCode String @length(4, 40, "A tracking code is 4 to 40 characters")
                      @label("Tracking")
}

model Order {
  id             Int          @id
  reference      String       @length(3, 20, "A reference is 3 to 20 characters, like ORD-1001") @unique @upper
  status         OrderStatus  @default(pending)

  
  /// ─── What was charged, and why ──────────────────────────────────────────
  ///
  /// Nine columns for one number, and every one of them is a copy of something
  /// that lives somewhere else — the code, its wording, the method, the rate.
  /// `OrderLine`'s header argues the general case; the specific one is that a
  /// merchant changes the VAT rate the week after a sale and every historical
  /// receipt silently reprices itself if the rate is joined rather than
  /// written down.
  ///
  /// The arithmetic is `api/src/pricing.ts` and nothing here recomputes it:
  ///
  ///     subtotal − discount + shipping + tax = total
  ///
  /// Every one is `@money(USD)`, which is to say a whole number of CENTS. The
  /// scale is the currency's and not the author's — `@frontierjs/toolbelt/units`
  /// is what knows the yen has none — and storing the minor unit is what makes
  /// the identity below an equality rather than a tolerance: five integers add
  /// up exactly, where five binary floats add up to within half a cent and the
  /// receipt has to be believed rather than checked.
  ///
  /// Rounding happens once, on the server, at the moment of sale, and only
  /// where a rate is applied — tax is the one component that is a
  /// multiplication. A screen that re-derived any line of this would round on
  /// its own and be a penny out on exactly the orders where a penny is noticed.
  ///
  /// Every one is `@system`: the shop computes them, and there is no request in
  /// which a caller stating their own total is anything but an attack. They are
  /// `@default(0)` rather than nullable because an order with no discount was
  /// discounted by zero, which is a fact — where null would mean *nobody has
  /// worked it out yet*, and that state does not exist after checkout.
  
  /// The lines, added up. What `OrderLine.lineTotal` sums to.
  subtotal       Int          @money(USD) @default(0) @gte(0) @system @label("Subtotal")
  
  /// What came off, and the code that took it off. The code is nullable
  /// because most orders have none; `discount` is 0 for those, and the two
  /// must agree — a row with a code and a zero is a bug worth being able to
  /// find.
  discountCode   String?      @system @label("Code")
  discountLabel  String?      @system @label("Discount")
  discount       Int          @money(USD) @default(0) @gte(0) @system @label("Discount amount")
  
  /// What was charged to send it, and what the shopper picked. A label of
  /// `Standard` against a zero is the free-over threshold having been met, and
  /// that reads correctly on a receipt without a fourth column to say so.
  shippingLabel  String?      @system @label("Shipping")
  shipping       Int          @money(USD) @default(0) @gte(0) @system @label("Shipping cost")
  
  /// Money that was never the shop's. The RATE is stored beside the amount
  /// because a receipt has to be checkable: an auditor reading `$8.40` needs
  /// the 0.2 it came from, and the row in `TaxRate` may have moved since.
  taxLabel       String?      @system @label("Tax")
  taxRate        Float        @default(0) @gte(0) @system @label("Rate")
  tax            Int          @money(USD) @default(0) @gte(0) @system @label("Tax amount")
  
  /// What the card was charged. The sum of the five above, stored rather than
  /// derived, and the only one of them a payment is ever compared against.
  ///
  /// The ONE money column here that is not `@system`, and the exception is
  /// deliberate: staff raise an order by hand for a sale that happened on the
  /// telephone, and that order has a total and no itemisation — no lines, and
  /// therefore no breakdown to add up. A screen renders the five above only
  /// where there are lines to explain, which is the same condition under which
  /// they were computed at all.
  total          Int          @money(USD) @default(0) @gte(0)
  
  note           String?
  
  /// belongsTo. `customerId` is emitted as a plain integer, so the browser only
  /// knows it is a reference because x-relations says so — that is what turns
  /// it into a picker instead of a number spinner.
  ///
  /// @label is what a human calls this field; @required carries the wording for
  /// the one rule that has nowhere else to live (required-ness is the absence
  /// of `?`, not an attribute). Both reach the browser AND the server from
  /// here — the form, the client-side check and the API all say this sentence.
  customerId     Int          @label("Customer")
  @required("Please select a customer from the list")
  customer       Customer     @relation(fields: [customerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  
  /// Written by a JOB, not by a request. `ship` books a courier, that call is
  /// slow and can fail, so it happens after the response: the action queues
  /// `book-courier` (@frontierjs/caravan) and the job patches this column when
  /// the courier answers. Nullable because between the move and the booking
  /// there is a real window where a shipped order has no tracking yet.
  ///
  /// It is the field that proves deferred work reaches the browser — the job
  /// writes through the orders SERVICE, so the write announces on the channel
  /// and a tab nobody touched fills this cell in seconds later.
  // Booked by the courier job (api/jobs/book-courier.job.ts), never by a
  // person. `@system` is what says so: readable by anyone, refused on write to
  // every caller but the application itself, which names the column on the
  // call. Without it the generated form offered a text box whose value a worker
  // overwrote a second later, and the page carried a hand-written exclusion
  // list to stop it (FJS-095).
  trackingCode   String?      @system @label("Tracking")
  
  /// What was bought. An order that carries only a total is a receipt with the
  /// itemisation torn off — see `model OrderLine` for what that cost.
  lines          OrderLine[]
  
  createdAt      DateTime     @default(now())
  
  /// ─── Removal ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ///
  /// The orders screen has a Delete button behind level 5, and until this column
  /// it destroyed the row and cascaded into `OrderLine` — an itemised financial
  /// record gone on one mis-click behind a two-button popover. `(cascade)`
  /// stamps the lines with the same instant, so an order and what it was made of
  /// leave together and come back together.
  ///
  /// It does not reach `InventoryMovement`: the ledger hangs off the VARIANT and
  /// not off the order, and what left the shelf did not stop having happened
  /// because a receipt was withdrawn.
  ///
  /// `remove()` does not consult `@@transitions` — stamping this column is a SQL
  /// UPDATE and not a state change — so nothing here stops a `pending` order
  /// being removed. Deliberate: the state machine says which states follow which,
  /// and whether a row may leave the list is an access question.
  deletedAt      DateTime?
  
  /// The state machine, declared where every other access rule lives rather
  /// than in whichever service handler was written first. Litestone enforces
  /// it at the Data boundary with an optimistic-lock WHERE, and it reaches the
  /// browser as x-transitions keyed by field — so the UI can render exactly
  /// the buttons that are legal from the row's current state.
  ///
  /// Refund is gated at 5. That gate can only live here, on the model: two
  /// models sharing OrderStatus are allowed to disagree about who may refund.
  @@transitions(status,
    pay:    pending         -> paid,
    ship:   paid            -> shipped,
    refund: paid            -> refunded @gate(5),
    cancel: [pending, paid] -> cancelled
  )
  
  /// Whose order this is, when the buyer had an account at the moment of sale.
  ///
  /// DENORMALISED from `Customer.userId`, deliberately. A row policy compares
  /// columns on its own model against `auth()` and does not traverse a
  /// relation, and `@from` crosses one only to AGGREGATE (`count: true`) — so
  /// *the orders of the customer this account is linked to* has nowhere to be
  /// written except here. The alternative was a service filter, which is a rule
  /// living outside the boundary that enforces every other rule about this row.
  ///
  /// Null for a guest sale, and it stays null: an account registered later
  /// takes the customer record with it, and the orders under it, through the
  /// policy on `Customer` — but an order already written is a receipt, and
  /// back-filling receipts is a write nobody asked for.
  userId         String?      @system
  
  @@gate("1.4.4.5")
  
  /// Staff read every order; a shopper reads their own. Two allows rather than
  /// one expression, because within an operation they are OR'd — and because
  /// they are two different sentences that will change at different times.
  @@allow('read', auth().isStaff)
  @@allow('read', userId == auth().id)
  
  /// ─── The receipt adds up ────────────────────────────────────────────────
  ///
  /// `subtotal − discount + shipping + tax = total`, the identity this model's
  /// header states in prose and `api/src/pricing.ts` computes. Until it could be
  /// declared it was true by everyone remembering — a job, a migration or an
  /// `asSystem()` repair that touched one column and not the rest left a receipt
  /// that did not add up, and nothing said so.
  ///
  /// A tolerance rather than equality, and it is not caution: fuzzed over 4,000
  /// real breakdowns from `priceBasket` — every discount kind, five tax rates,
  /// the free-over threshold either side — the worst drift between `total` and
  /// SQLite's own sum of the components was 7.3e-12, and it was never zero. So
  /// equality would fail on live data. Half a penny is nine orders of magnitude
  /// above the noise and an order of magnitude below the smallest real error,
  /// which is one penny.
  ///
  /// `subtotal = 0` is the exemption, and it is the shape of an order staff
  /// raised by hand: a total, and no lines to itemise. What it cannot say is
  /// *no lines* — a CHECK may not hold a subquery, which is the same wall
  /// `@@fts`'s membership note runs into further up this file.
  @@check("subtotal = 0 OR total = subtotal - discount + shipping + tax",
          "the breakdown does not add up to the total")
  
  /// Nothing may take off more than was there. `pricing.ts` caps a fixed-amount
  /// code at the subtotal in both directions; this is the same rule, said where
  /// a write that did not come through the checkout still meets it.
  @@check("discount <= subtotal",
          "a discount cannot be larger than the subtotal")
  
  @@softDelete(cascade)
  
  @@log(audit)
}

/// What was bought, at the price it was bought for.
///
/// ─── Why the columns are copies ──────────────────────────────────────────
///
/// Every column below except the two ids is a value this row already has a
/// relation to. That is not denormalisation for speed — it is that a line is a
/// statement about a MOMENT and its neighbours are statements about now. A
/// variant's price is what the shop charges today; `unitPrice` is what this
/// shopper was charged, and re-reading the first to render the second rewrites
/// what past customers paid every time somebody edits the catalogue. Same for
/// the wording: `description` is the sentence that was on the screen, and a
/// colourway renamed next year does not un-sell this one.
///
/// The relation stays for the one thing a copy cannot do — link back to the
/// product page — and it is `Restrict` for the reason `InventoryMovement`'s is:
/// history that a DELETE somewhere else can empty is not history.
///
/// ─── This does not replace the ledger, and the two are not the same fact ──
///
/// `InventoryMovement` records what left the SHELF; these record what was
/// SOLD. A refund still reads the movements back (`api/src/inventory.ts`
/// `restock`), because the question there is what physically moved and the
/// signed tape is the only thing that answers it. The question here is what
/// the shop billed for, which the tape cannot answer: it carries no prices.
model OrderLine {
  id           Int             @id
  orderId      Int             @label("Order")
  order        Order           @relation(fields: [orderId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  
  variantId    Int             @label("Variant")
  variant      ProductVariant  @relation(fields: [variantId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  
  /// The three copies. `sku` is what a picker in the warehouse reads,
  /// `description` is what the customer read, and neither is looked up again.
  sku          String          @length(1, 40)
  description  String          @length(1, 160) @label("Item")
  
  quantity     Int             @gte(1) @lte(99)
  unitPrice    Int             @money(USD) @gte(0) @label("Unit price")
  
  /// STORED, not multiplied at read time — and this is the column that decides
  /// whether the lines add up to the total. In cents the multiplication is
  /// exact, which removes the penny this column was written for and leaves the
  /// other reason: a screen that recomputed `unitPrice * quantity` would be
  /// quoting today's price for a sale that happened last week the moment
  /// anything above it changes.
  lineTotal    Int             @money(USD) @gte(0) @label("Total")
  
  /// A FOURTH copy, and the one that is not about the sale.
  ///
  /// The three above are copies because a price and a description are facts
  /// about the moment of sale. This one is a copy for a duller reason: a row
  /// policy compares columns on ITS OWN model against `auth()` and cannot name
  /// one on a related model — `@@allow('read', order.userId == auth().id)` is a
  /// parse error at the dot (`FJS-499`). So *the lines of my own orders* has
  /// nowhere to live but here.
  ///
  /// Stated rather than hidden, because the cost is real: this column and
  /// `Order.userId` can disagree, and nothing in the schema can stop them.
  /// `carts.checkout` writes both inside one transaction, which is the whole of
  /// what keeps them together.
  userId       String?         @system
  
  createdAt    DateTime        @default(now())
  
  /// Stamped by `Order`'s cascade and by nothing else — there is no path that
  /// removes a line on its own, the same way there is none that writes one.
  deletedAt    DateTime?
  
  /// Read where the order reads; write only through `asSystem()`.
  ///
  /// A line has no caller-facing write path at all — `carts.checkout` is the
  /// one thing that makes one, as the shop recording its own sale, and nothing
  /// ever edits one afterwards. So the write levels are 8 rather than 5: an
  /// administrator has nothing to say to this table either, and putting 5 here
  /// would offer a generated form for editing what a customer was charged.
  ///
  /// Read was 0. That is the catalogue's answer and this is the sales ledger:
  /// every SKU, every quantity and every price the shop has ever charged,
  /// answerable to anyone with `curl`. Same paste as `Order` and `Customer`.
  @@gate("1.8.8.8")
  
  @@allow('read', auth().isStaff)
  @@allow('read', userId == auth().id)
  
  @@softDelete
  
  /// Every read of this table is "the lines of one order".
  @@index([orderId])
}

// ─── Payments ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The money, and the one thing in this shop the shop does not decide. Every
// row below is written because a PROVIDER said something — never because a
// caller asked — and that is what the gates say: read at ADMINISTRATOR(5),
// write only through `asSystem()`, which is the shop recording what it was
// told. A shopper never touches either table; they learn the answer from the
// order, whose status is the thing they actually care about.

/// `refunded` means FULLY refunded and nothing else. A partly refunded payment
/// stays `succeeded` and carries the amount that went back — those are two
/// facts and a fifth enum member would fuse them, so the one question a shop
/// asks constantly ("is this settled?") would need to name two states.
enum PaymentStatus { pending succeeded failed refunded }

/// One attempt to take money for one order.
///
/// ─── Why `providerRef` is @unique and is not the id ──────────────────────
///
/// A webhook arrives naming the PROVIDER's id and nothing else — it has never
/// heard of this database. So the column the lookup goes through has to be the
/// provider's, and it has to be unique or a redelivery could settle a second
/// row. It is not the primary key because a provider id is theirs to change
/// the shape of, and a foreign key pointing at a string somebody else mints is
/// a migration waiting to happen.
model Payment {
  id Int @id

  providerRef String @unique @length(3, 64) @label("Provider reference")

  status PaymentStatus @default(pending)

  /// COPIED from the order at the moment the intent was created, not joined.
  /// What was authorised is a fact about that authorisation, and an order
  /// whose total was corrected afterwards must not silently rewrite what the
  /// provider was asked for — the disagreement between the two numbers is the
  /// thing a person needs to see.
  ///
  /// `@money(field: currency)` and not `@money(USD)`: this is the one table in
  /// the shop that already stores a code per row, because a provider is asked
  /// in a currency and answers in one. The scale is therefore that ROW's — a
  /// JPY intent is a whole number of yen and a USD one is cents — which is
  /// exactly the fact a shared `@money(USD)` here would get wrong by a
  /// hundred.
  amount   Int    @money(field: currency) @gte(0)
  currency String @default("USD") @length(3, 3) @upper

  orderId Int   @label("Order")
  order   Order @relation(fields: [orderId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  /// How much has gone back, of `amount`. A number rather than a flag because a
  /// refund is not one event: a shop refunds the postage today and the item
  /// next week, and each is a separate call to the provider with its own
  /// ledger row. The order only moves when this reaches `amount`.
  refundedAmount Int @money(field: currency) @default(0) @gte(0)

  /// The provider's words, kept verbatim. Nothing branches on it — it is for
  /// the person reading the row, and inventing a taxonomy over somebody else's
  /// failure strings is a mapping that goes stale without ever failing.
  failureReason String? @length(0, 200)

  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  settledAt DateTime?

  /// One live attempt per order at a time is a rule this shop does not need:
  /// a failed card followed by a good one is two rows, and the history is the
  /// point. The index is what makes "this order's payments" cheap.
  @@index([orderId])

  /// RCUD. Read 5 — an administrator; a payment names amounts and a provider
  /// reference. Create and update 8 — SYSTEM, because the only writer is the
  /// shop recording what a provider said, and a level is not what separates
  /// those: no PERSON at any standing writes one. Delete 9 — LOCKED. Money
  /// that was taken cannot stop having been taken, and 9 is how that is spelt
  /// for every caller including the application itself.
  @@gate("5.8.8.9")
  @@log(audit)
}

/// Every event the provider has delivered, once each. Append-only.
///
/// ─── What this table is for, and what it is NOT for ──────────────────────
///
/// It is NOT what makes paying an order idempotent. That is the state machine:
/// `pay` is `pending -> paid`, so a second one is refused at the Data boundary
/// whatever this table says. Nor is it the signature replay check, which is a
/// nonce inside a five-minute window and is gone the moment the window passes.
///
/// It is the answer to "what did they tell us, and when" — the row a person
/// reads when the order and the provider's dashboard disagree. The @unique is
/// what makes a redelivery cheap: the handler claims the id and stops, rather
/// than re-deriving that there is nothing to do.
///
/// The claim and the effect are written in ONE transaction (`transactional:`
/// on `payments.record`), which is the only arrangement that is correct. Claim
/// first and commit separately, and a crash in between leaves the event
/// claimed and the order unpaid — and the provider's retry is deduped away by
/// the very row that recorded nothing happening.
model PaymentEvent {
  id          Int       @id
  
  /// The provider's id FOR THE EVENT, not for the payment — one payment
  /// produces several, and deduping on the payment would drop the ones that
  /// come after the first.
  eventId     String    @unique @length(3, 80) @label("Event")
  
  kind        String    @length(1, 60) @label("Type")
  
  /// Which payment it was about, as the provider spelt it. A plain string and
  /// not a relation: an event may name a payment this shop has no row for
  /// (a provider-side refund, a test event fired from their dashboard), and a
  /// foreign key would refuse to record the one event most worth recording.
  paymentRef  String?   @length(0, 64) @label("Payment")
  
  receivedAt  DateTime  @default(now())
  
  /// Read 5, create 8, update and delete 9. Append-only, and LOCKED rather
  /// than SYSTEM for the two that would edit history: a ledger `asSystem()`
  /// can rewrite is a ledger.
  @@gate("5.8.9.9")
}

// ─── Basket ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

enum CartStatus { open ordered abandoned }

/// A basket, and the one model in this app owned by NOBODY.
///
/// A shopper is a stranger — no account, no session, level 0 — and a stranger
/// still has to be the only person who can see their own basket. That cannot
/// be `userId == auth().id`, because there is no `auth().id` to compare, and it
/// must not be a service reading through `asSystem()`, because access is
/// declared in the schema and not in hooks (Invariant 6).
///
/// So the owner is a BEARER TOKEN, and `api/cart-claim.ts` turns the header
/// carrying it into a claim on the principal before the Data boundary scopes
/// the client. `auth().cartToken` is then a claim a stranger holds, and the
/// policies below are ordinary row policies over it.
///
/// The token is `@guarded`: the app writes it, `asSystem()` reads it, and no
/// caller ever gets it back in a response — the browser knows it because it
/// was handed it once, at creation, by the service that minted it.
model Cart {
  id    Int    @id
  token String @unique @guarded @default(cuid())

  /// Set when a signed-in person owns the basket, null while they are a guest.
  /// Both audiences use the same policies — the token is what proves ownership
  /// either way, so signing in mid-shop does not lose the basket.
  userId String?

  status CartStatus @default(open)

  /// ─── The money, while it is still changeable ────────────────────────────
  ///
  /// Two references and no amounts. A basket stores WHICH code and WHICH
  /// method, and every figure is computed on read by `api/src/pricing.ts` —
  /// the opposite of `Order`, which stores the figures and no references.
  ///
  /// That inversion is the whole distinction between the two tables. A basket
  /// is a question the shop answers again on every request, so a rate that
  /// changed or a code that expired while the shopper was deciding must show
  /// up immediately; an order is a statement about a moment, so nothing about
  /// it may move afterwards. Storing amounts on the basket would quote a price
  /// the shop no longer offers, and storing references on the order would
  /// reprice history.
  ///
  /// Both are `@system`. The basket's own policy lets its holder update it —
  /// `@@allow('update', token == auth().cartToken)` — which is what makes the
  /// quantity stepper work without a session, and it would just as happily let
  /// them point `discountId` at any row in the table. So neither is set by a
  /// write: `carts.applyDiscount` takes a CODE and validates it, and
  /// `carts.setShipping` takes a method and checks it is active.
  discountId Int?      @system @label("Discount")
  discount   Discount? @relation(fields: [discountId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  shippingMethodId Int?            @system @label("Shipping")
  shippingMethod   ShippingMethod? @relation(fields: [shippingMethodId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)

  /// ─── Handing a basket to another ORIGIN ─────────────────────────────────
  ///
  /// An embeddable buy button runs on a page the shop does not own, so its
  /// basket token lives in THAT origin's storage and the shop's own site cannot
  /// read it. Sending the shopper to checkout therefore means handing the
  /// capability across an origin boundary, and the token itself must not be
  /// what travels: a URL is written to history, to a `Referer` header and to
  /// every log between here and there, and this one is a bearer secret good for
  /// the life of the basket.
  ///
  /// So what travels is a ONE-TIME CODE. `carts.handoff` mints it, the link
  /// carries it in the fragment (which is never sent to a server), and
  /// `carts.redeem` exchanges it for the token and clears it in the same write.
  /// It is worth one basket, for two minutes, once.
  ///
  /// `@guarded` for the same reason `token` is: the code is answered by exactly
  /// one method, to the caller already holding the basket.
  handoffCode    String?   @unique @guarded
  handoffExpires DateTime? @guarded

  lines CartLine[]
  /// The stock this basket is holding. A hold is NOT the line: the line is what
  /// the shopper chose, the hold is what the warehouse has set aside for them,
  /// and the second one expires on its own.
  holds StockReservation[]

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  /// Create at 0 — a stranger starting a basket is the entire point. Read and
  /// update at 0 too, because the POLICY is what scopes them; a gate answers
  /// *what kind of caller*, never *which rows*. Delete needs an admin: a
  /// basket is abandoned by the sweep, not removed by its holder.
  @@gate("0.0.0.5")

  @@allow('read',   token == auth().cartToken)
  @@allow('update', token == auth().cartToken)
}

/// One line. The quantity and the PRICE THE SHOPPER WAS SHOWN, which is not
/// the same fact as the variant's price today — a basket left overnight must
/// either honour what it quoted or say out loud that it changed, and it can do
/// neither if the number was never written down.
model CartLine {
  id         Int             @id
  cartId     Int             @label("Basket")
  cart       Cart            @relation(fields: [cartId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  
  variantId  Int             @label("Variant") @required("Pick a size and colour")
  variant    ProductVariant  @relation(fields: [variantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  
  quantity   Int             @gte(1) @lte(99) @default(1)
  unitPrice  Int             @money(USD) @gte(0) @system
  
  createdAt  DateTime        @default(now())
  
  /// One line per variant. A second "navy / m" is a quantity change, not a
  /// second row — and without this the basket shows the same thing twice and
  /// the two rows disagree about how many.
  @@unique([cartId, variantId])
  
  /// The basket's token, copied onto the line.
  ///
  /// A policy cannot traverse a relation — `cart.token == auth().cartToken`
  /// does not parse — so a child row that is scoped by its parent has to carry
  /// the parent's key itself. (`@@tenant(via: parent)` does exactly this for
  /// the tenancy feature; a plain `@@allow` has no equivalent.) It is written
  /// once, with the line, and a line never moves basket.
  ///
  /// NOT `@guarded`, unlike the copy on `Cart`. A guard locks BOTH directions,
  /// so the caller who has to write this column could not — measured, an
  /// `AccessDeniedError` on a guest adding a line to their own basket. It does
  /// not need one: the create policy below only admits the caller's own token,
  /// and reading it back tells the bearer a secret they are already holding.
  token      String
  
  /// All four at 0, unlike `Cart` above, and the difference is delete.
  ///
  /// A gate answers *what kind of caller* and a policy answers *which rows*.
  /// Taking a line out of a basket is an ordinary thing a shopper does, and the
  /// shopper is a stranger — so the gate has nothing to say and the policy
  /// below is the whole of the protection. `Cart` keeps 5 for delete because
  /// removing a BASKET is not something its holder does: it is abandoned by the
  /// sweep.
  ///
  /// This read 5 for delete for as long as the basket existed, with the allow
  /// beneath it saying what was meant. Nothing noticed, because nothing removed
  /// a line: `removeLine` and `setQuantity(0)` both answered 403 to every guest
  /// in the shop, and the two drives that fill a basket only ever added to it.
  @@gate("0.0.0.0")
  @@allow('read',   token == auth().cartToken)
  @@allow('create', token == auth().cartToken)
  @@allow('update', token == auth().cartToken)
  @@allow('delete', token == auth().cartToken)
}

/// What `carts.checkout` accepts. A basket knows what is in it; it does not
/// know who is buying, and a guest has no Customer row until this moment.
///
/// A `type` rather than a model for the same reason `TrackingUpdate` is one:
/// there is nothing to store. It describes only what may arrive, and naming it
/// in the service's `methods:` gets it the same generated JSON Schema, the same
/// wording and the same 400 a model create would get — which is the half a
/// custom method otherwise has to do by hand with a cast.
/// The payload `carts.applyDiscount` accepts.
///
/// A `type` and not a model for `TrackingUpdate`'s reason — there is nothing to
/// store, only something to validate. What it buys is that a three-character
/// minimum and the sentence explaining it are declared in the seed beside every
/// other rule, so the 400 a mistyped code gets is generated rather than written
/// by hand in the service and forgotten when the column changes.
type DiscountCode {
  code String @length(3, 20, "A discount code is 3 to 20 characters") @upper
              @label("Discount code")
              @required("Enter the code you were given")
}

/// The payload `carts.setShipping` accepts. Nullable on purpose: choosing no
/// method is a legitimate choice — it is how a shopper goes back to undecided —
/// and a separate `clearShipping` method for one null would be a second name
/// for one idea.
type ShippingChoice {
  shippingMethodId Int? @label("Shipping")
}

type CheckoutDetails {
  email String @email @lower @label("Email")
                @required("We need an email address to send the receipt to")
  name  String @length(2, 80) @label("Name")
                @required("Please give a name for the order")
  note  String? @length(0, 500) @label("Order note")
}

// ─── Inventory ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Two tables and one number that is neither of them.
//
//   ProductVariant.stock   ON HAND   — what the warehouse could pick right now
//   StockReservation       HELD      — set aside for a basket, with an expiry
//   available              stock − the unexpired holds, computed, never stored
//
// The whole feature is that third line. A shop that offers what it has ON HAND
// sells the last one twice: two shoppers both see `1 left`, both add it, and
// the second finds out at the till. A shop that decrements on ADD instead sells
// it zero times — an abandoned basket takes stock off the shelf forever, and
// there is nothing left to put it back.
//
// So adding to a basket takes a HOLD with a clock on it, and `api/inventory.ts`
// is the one module that reads or writes any of the three.

/// Why the shelf moved. An adjustment with no reason is a number nobody can
/// audit, and `adjusted` alone would make a stocktake correction and a theft
/// look the same in the ledger.
enum StockMovementKind { received sold returned adjusted damaged }

/// Stock set aside for one basket, until a moment.
///
/// ─── Why this is a row and not a column on CartLine ──────────────────────
///
/// It would fit there — a line already names a variant and a quantity, and an
/// `heldUntil` column would have been three characters of schema. It is wrong
/// for one reason and the reason is a POLICY: CartLine is scoped by the
/// shopper's token (`@@allow('read', token == auth().cartToken)`), and
/// availability is a sum over *everybody's* holds. Summing CartLine from a
/// shopper's own client answers a sum over their own basket — a number that is
/// always plausible, usually zero, and never the one asked for. It is the exact
/// shape the house rule warns about: a wrong policy is an empty screen, not an
/// error.
///
/// A hold is a fact about the SHELF, so it is a table about the shelf, and the
/// gate says who may look: an administrator reads them (that is the inventory
/// screen), and nothing below `asSystem()` writes one. There is no row policy
/// because there is no caller-facing read — the shopper learns about their own
/// hold from the basket the `carts` service builds for them.
model StockReservation {
  id Int @id

  variantId Int            @label("Variant")
  variant   ProductVariant @relation(fields: [variantId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  cartId Int  @label("Basket")
  cart   Cart @relation(fields: [cartId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  quantity Int @gte(1) @lte(99)

  /// When the shelf takes it back. THE READ IS THE TRUTH: every availability
  /// sum filters on this instant, so a hold is dead the moment it passes even
  /// if no sweep has run. `release-holds` deletes the rows, which keeps the
  /// table small and is housekeeping — correctness must not depend on a cron
  /// having fired, because then a queue outage quietly stops the shop selling.
  expiresAt DateTime

  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// One hold per basket per shelf, mirroring the line it stands for.
  @@unique([cartId, variantId])

  /// Every availability sum is `(variantId, expiresAt)` — the one query this
  /// table exists to answer, and the one on the path of every add to basket.
  @@index([variantId, expiresAt])

  @@gate("5.8.8.8")
}

/// Why the shelf is at the number it is at. Append-only.
///
/// `stock` is the running total and this is the tape behind it: nothing writes
/// that column without writing a row here in the same breath, which is what
/// makes the two reconcilable at all. It is not the audit trail — `@@log(audit)`
/// records that SOMEBODY changed a row and Litestone owns its format. This
/// records what happened to the SHELF, in the shop's own words, and a customer
/// service agent reads it.
///
/// ─── The gate is "5.5.9.9" and each digit is deliberate ──────────────────
///
///   read   5  an administrator; a movement names orders and quantities
///   create 5  receiving stock is an administrator's act, so the Data boundary
///             is what refuses it — `inventory.receive` contains no check of
///             its own and needs none
///   update 9  LOCKED — nothing passes 9, `asSystem()` included
///   delete 9  LOCKED
///
/// 9 is what "append-only" is spelled with. A comment saying the same thing is
/// a comment; this is enforced at the Data boundary for every caller including
/// the application itself, which is the only version of the promise worth
/// having.
///
/// The one movement an ADMINISTRATOR does not write is `sold`: that one is
/// written by a shopper at level 0 checking out, so `carts.checkout` makes it
/// through `asSystem()` — the shop recording a sale on its own behalf. Which
/// client a `move()` is handed is therefore a real decision at every call site,
/// and it is why `api/inventory.ts` takes one rather than reaching for a global.
model InventoryMovement {
  id           Int                @id
  
  variantId    Int                @label("Variant")
  /// Restrict, not Cascade. A shelf that has ever moved cannot be deleted out
  /// from under its own ledger — retiring a variant is `active: false`, which
  /// is what a shop means by "we do not sell this any more". The alternative
  /// is an append-only table a DELETE on another table can empty.
  variant      ProductVariant     @relation(fields: [variantId], references: [id], onDelete: Restrict)
  
  kind         StockMovementKind
  
  /// SIGNED, and it is the movement rather than the new total: +12 received,
  /// −2 sold. A ledger of totals cannot be summed, and summing is the only
  /// thing anybody ever wants to do with one.
  quantity     Int
  
  /// Both ends, written down. A movement that says only "−2" is unreadable the
  /// moment two of them race; with both, a row is checkable on its own and a
  /// gap between one row's `after` and the next one's `before` is visible.
  stockBefore  Int                @gte(0)
  stockAfter   Int                @gte(0)
  
  /// What it was for. An order reference for a sale, a delivery note for a
  /// receipt, blank for a stocktake correction that only has the note.
  reference    String?            @length(1, 40)
  note         String?            @length(0, 200)
  
  createdAt    DateTime           @default(now())
  
  @@gate("5.5.9.9")
}

/// What `inventory.receive` accepts — a delivery arriving at the warehouse.
///
/// A `type` and not a model for the reason `TrackingUpdate` and
/// `CheckoutDetails` are: there is nothing to store. What gets stored is the
/// InventoryMovement this produces, and its shape is not this one — `quantity`
/// here is what turned up, and there it is signed and sits beside both ends of
/// the shelf it moved.
type StockReceipt {
  variantId Int @label("Variant") @required("Which shelf did this arrive on?")
  quantity  Int @gte(1, "A delivery is at least one item")
                @lte(10000, "More than ten thousand in one delivery is a typo more often than it is a pallet")
                @label("How many")
  reference String? @length(1, 40) @label("Delivery note")
  note      String? @length(0, 200) @label("Note")
}

/// What `inventory.adjust` accepts — a stocktake correction, a breakage, a
/// return to the shelf.
///
/// `quantity` is SIGNED here and required to be, because the three reasons go
/// in different directions and a form with a separate up/down control would let
/// somebody file a breakage that added stock. `note` is required, which
/// `receive`'s is not: a delivery explains itself and a correction does not.
type StockAdjustment {
  variantId Int @label("Variant") @required("Which shelf is being corrected?")
  quantity  Int @gte(-10000) @lte(10000) @label("Change")
                @required("How many, and which way — negative takes stock off the shelf")
  kind StockMovementKind @label("Reason")
                         @required("Say why the number is moving")
  note String @length(3, 200) @label("Note")
              @required("An adjustment with no reason is a number nobody can audit")
}

// ─── Notifications ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `fli add notifications` writes this model; it is here by hand because this
// app appends auth's fragments at runtime and keeps its own models in one file.
//
// A notification is not a shop noun — it is the SHOP TALKING TO ITS STAFF, so
// the row belongs to a User (auth's uuid `id`, hence String) and never to a
// Customer. The customer hears about their order by email, which persists
// nothing here; the two audiences are two notification classes, not one class
// with a branch in it.

/// What a notification is ABOUT, for the row it links to. A pair like this
/// carries no foreign key — nothing cascades, nothing joins, and a deleted
/// order leaves its notifications behind as dead links, which is what this app
/// wants (see `OrderPaid.ts`). What the pair CAN be told is which models it may
/// name, and this is it: a value outside the set is refused by a table CHECK,
/// so a migration, a seed and `asSystem()` are all held to it too, and a
/// generated form gets a picker instead of a text box.
///
/// Add a member when a notification class starts pointing somewhere new.
enum NotificationContext { Order }

model Notification {
  id           Int        @id
  userId       String
  type         String
  data         Json
  contextType  NotificationContext?
  contextId    Int?
  readAt       DateTime?
  createdAt    DateTime   @default(now())
  
  /// RCUD: read is open because the row policy below is what scopes it —
  /// a gate is per-operation and cannot say "your own rows". Create is 8
  /// (SYSTEM): only `asSystem()` writes one, which is what the inApp driver
  /// uses. NOT 9 — that is LOCKED, which `asSystem()` does not pass either,
  /// so a 9 here would stop the driver from ever writing a row. Update is 4
  /// so a signed-in user can mark their own as read; delete is SYSTEM.
  @@gate("0.8.4.8")
  @@allow('read',   userId == auth().id)
  @@allow('update', userId == auth().id)
}

// ─── What is NOT in this file ─────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `extend model User { isStaff … }` lives in db/user.lite and is assembled by
// api/src/core/db.ts, not imported here. This file is read by the browser build
// alone, where auth's `User` does not exist and an extend naming it is refused
// by name — which empties the schema registry and makes every generated form
// stop working, with a parse error nobody is looking at as the only clue.
